<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Weilburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Weilburg is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It was the residence of the counts and princes of Nassau-Weilburg from the 14th century, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> palace with baroque additions dominates the town. The Weilburg line went on to rule the Duchy of Nassau and, from 1890, the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/weilburg.jpg" alt="Weilburg" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fritz Geller-Grimm · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weilburg_Luftbild_058.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Weilburg lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a> valley between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westerwald">Westerwald</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a>, just upstream from where the Weil flows into the Lahn, 80 km southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>. The Old Town stands on and around a rocky hill that the Lahn almost encircles. The earliest traces of settlement nearby are thought to be La Tène finds from the Scheuernberger Kopf near Kirschhofen. The site gave control of the Lahn and of two long-distance routes, the Hohe Straße from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and the Via Publica from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Weilburg was first mentioned in 906 in a chronicle by Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regino-of-prum">Regino of Prüm</a> as a fortification called Wilineburch. King Conrad I founded a church and abbey there, and the St. Walpurgis monastery was founded in 912. In 918, on his deathbed at the castle, Conrad recommended that his brother Eberhard deliver the imperial insignia to his bitterest rival, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> duke Heinrich, an episode known as the Weilburger Testament. From 993 to 1062 the town was gradually given to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishopric-of-worms">Bishopric of Worms</a>, which about 1225 pledged overlordship to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a>.</p><p>Count Johann I of Nassau made his residence there in 1355, turning the castle into a palace and building the town fortifications, and in 1359 a stone bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a>. Count Johann Ernst, who lived from 1664 to 1719, expanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> high palace and laid out a park, so that Weilburg became one of the best preserved small German residence towns of the age of absolutism. From 1806 it was the seat of government of the new Duchy of Nassau, until Duke William moved his residence to Biebrich in 1816. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed the duchy in 1866, and in 1871 it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Weilburg has just under 13,000 inhabitants and is the third largest town of the Limburg-Weilburg district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. Schloss Weilburg, dating to the 14th century with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> parts, takes in almost half the Old Town and has been a museum since 1935, when Charlotte of Nassau-Weilburg, Grand Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, sold it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. The Luxembourg ruling house kept the burial chapel of its ancestors. The town is also known for loam buildings in the Pisee style and for its Gymnasium Philippinum, which grew out of a school founded in 1540.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Weilburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Walramian line of the house of Nassau held lands south of the Lahn, and a partition in 1344 gave Count John I a share centred on <b>Weilburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau-weilburg">County of Nassau-Weilburg</a></li><li><blockquote>When he died without sons in 1816, the Usingen line ended and Nassau passed to the <b>Weilburg</b> line.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-nassau-usingen">Principality of Nassau-Usingen</a></li></ul></section>
